course: dictionary
lesson: 2648
title: Determiners: the little words before nouns
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A determiner is a small word that comes before a noun. It tells you which one, whose one, or how many. «The» and «a» are determiners, and so are «this», «my», «some», «every» and numbers like «three».
explain: Determiners come first in a noun phrase, before any adjectives. In «the big red bus», «the» is the determiner, «big» and «red» describe the bus, and «bus» is the noun.
explain: Some determiners show whose thing it is, like «my», «your», «his», «her», «our» and «their». These are called possessive determiners. «My» and «your» go before a noun, but «mine» and «yours» stand alone, so they are pronouns, not determiners.
fact: A determiner goes before a noun to show which one or how many.
fact: Examples of determiners: the, a, an, this, that, some, every, my, your, two.
fact: Possessive determiners such as «my», «your» and «their» come before a noun: «my bike», «their house».
fact: In «The hungry fox ate quickly», «the» is a determiner, «hungry» is an adjective and «quickly» is an adverb.
fact: A determiner always goes before any adjectives: «the big red bus», not «big the red bus».
exercise: Find the determiner in «The big red bus stopped.» | red | *The | big | stopped
exercise: Which word tells you how many in «Some children ran to the park.»? | ran | *Some | park | to
exercise: Choose the best word: «___ cat sleeps on my bed.» | Under | Quickly | Happily | *Our
test: Which word is a possessive determiner in «My best friend lives next door.»? | best | *My | lives | friend
test: Which word is a possessive determiner in «Their dog is very old.»? | very | dog | old | *Their
exam: Which word is a determiner in «The hungry fox ate quickly.»? | quickly | fox | *The | hungry
